sections.body:
Related pages often need to share the same surrounding layout -- a settings section with a persistent sidebar, where only the main content area changes between `/settings/profile` and `/settings/billing`. **Nested routes** let a parent route render that shared layout once, with a placeholder (typically an `<Outlet />`) marking where the matching child route's content should appear:

```jsx
function SettingsLayout() {
  return (
    <div className="settings-layout">
      <SettingsSidebar />
      <Outlet />
    </div>
  );
}

<Route path="/settings" element={<SettingsLayout />}>
  <Route path="profile" element={<ProfileSettings />} />
  <Route path="billing" element={<BillingSettings />} />
</Route>
```

Navigating between `/settings/profile` and `/settings/billing` keeps SettingsLayout (and therefore SettingsSidebar) mounted the whole time -- only whatever renders inside `<Outlet />` changes. Without nesting, each top-level page would have to duplicate the sidebar and surrounding markup itself, and that duplicated layout would need to be kept in sync by hand every time it changed.

